Elspeth woke before the rooster, which was usual, and knew someone was coming, which was not.

She lay still under the wool blanket and listened. The Oak and Ember creaked the way it always did at night. Old timber settling. Wind in the chimney. Nothing on the stairs. Nothing at the door. Still she knew, the way she knew when bread was done without opening the oven. A feeling in the backs of her hands.

She dressed in the dark. Kerchief, apron, sleeves rolled to the elbow. The stairs smelled of oak and of her father, the way they always would. She touched the rail at the bottom, where his hand had worn the wood smooth. She did that every morning. She had stopped noticing years ago.

--

The fire was low. She knelt and fed it. Two splits of pine, one of oak. She did not blow on it. She never had to. She only waited, and the flames climbed the oak as though they had been waiting for her too.

Father had laughed at that. "Fire likes you, Ellie," he had said. "Always did. Your mother was the same." Then he would cough, and wink, and ask what was for breakfast.

She hung the pots on the cranes. Barley in one. Water for the stew in the other. Then she went to the kitchen and pulled the bread trough out from under the table.

She did not measure. She had never measured. Father had measured. He had a set of tin cups nested one inside the other, and he would scrape each one flat with the back of a knife. She still had them, on a nail by the door. She did not use them. Her hands knew.

Flour. Water, warm from the kettle. Salt. The starter from the crock, which smelled sour and alive. She worked it until it pushed back.

Then she stopped. She looked at the dough.

It wanted something else.

She took down the jar of seeds. Then the walnuts, which she had been saving for the harvest loaves. Then a pinch of dried rosemary, and thyme, and after a moment, a little sage. She did not know why the sage. Sage was for the dead. Sage was for funerals and for keeping flies from a body. She nearly put it back.

She kneaded it in anyway.

Someone was going somewhere dark today. The bread wanted to go with him.

She covered the dough with a cloth and set it near the hearth. It began to rise almost at once. It always did, when she was thinking of someone.

--

She heard the step before the latch.

Heavy boots, but placed carefully. A man who did not want to wake anyone. A man who had gotten used to walking among things that might wake. She knew that step. She had not heard it inside the Oak and Ember for a very long time.

She did not turn around. She kept her eyes on the pot and the ladle moving. If she turned around, he would see her face, and he would leave.

The door opened. Cold air. Coal dust and something underneath it, sweet and wrong. She had smelled it on him for a year. Death had a smell, and he wore it like a coat he could not take off.

The door closed. He did not sit.

"You look thin," she said.

She tapped the ladle on the pot and moved to stir the second one. The fire had come up too high while she listened for him. She nudged it with the poker. It calmed. "Sit."

"I know it's early --"

"Sit."

She nudged a bench with her foot and looked at him over her shoulder. Just a look. She smiled with her eyes, because if she smiled with her mouth he would think she pitied him, and he would go.

He sat in the far corner. As far from the fire as he could get. As far from her as he could get. He folded himself small, like a man who had forgotten he was allowed to take up room.

She went to the kitchen. The bread had risen and baked while she wasn't looking. That happened sometimes. She didn't think about it. She broke it while it was still too hot to touch and set it on a plate with a wedge of the good cheese, the crumbly white one, and wiped the edge of the plate with her apron, and wiped the table as she set it down.

"Eat."

He broke the bread. Steam came out of the middle of it. She watched him smell it before he tasted it. She watched his shoulders drop half an inch.

She leaned on the table on her elbows and did not stare. She had learned from Father how to watch a man eat without making him feel watched. You looked at his hands, not his face. You let him have his face.

His hands were raw. Split along the knuckles. Gray in the creases, the gray that never came out. There was a scab on the back of one wrist, shaped like a crescent. Like a fingernail. Like something had clawed him.

He ate fast. Then he caught himself eating fast and swallowed, embarrassed. She pretended not to see.

"You're not here for breakfast," she said.

"No."

Of course he wasn't. He hadn't been here for breakfast in a year. He was here because he was going to the mine, and some part of him had wanted someone to know. She had known since before the rooster.

"Have another piece, then."

She broke the rest of the loaf and pushed it into his hands.

"Have the lot. You'll need it. Where you're going."

"You don't have to --"

"I do," she said. "For the same reason you do."

He stared at her. His eyes were brown and very tired, with deep smudges under them, and for a moment she saw the boy who had once stolen apples from her father's cellar and confessed to it the next day, red to the ears. Then the boy was gone, and the Bone Man was back.

"And what reason is that?"

She had not meant to answer. It came out anyway.

"So things don't fall apart all at once."

She went to the kitchen before he could see her face. She stood with her back to the wall and her hands flat on the flour board and breathed. Behind her, she heard him stand. She heard the soft chink of coins on the plate. She heard his step toward the door.

She made herself come back out.

"Matthis."

He stopped.

"It's been a year," she said. "Since you've been back inside."

He looked at her. He knew what she meant. Not the pub. The mine.

"Elspeth."

She brushed the flour from her hands. "Yes?"

He gestured at the plate. "Thank you."

She smiled. Not wide. Just enough. Just as much as he could carry.

"Come back afterward," she said. "You'll want supper."

He went. The door closed. The cold air faded.

She picked up the coins and put them in the jar on the mantle that she never spent from. It was nearly full. Most of it was his.

--

The miners came at dawn and the Oak and Ember filled up like a lung.

She knew them by their coughs before she knew them by their faces. Old Hab with the wet one, deep in his chest. She put honey in his tea without asking, and a slice of onion, and he grumbled about it and drank it. By the time he left, he was breathing easier. It was the honey, she thought. Honey was good for the chest.

Brim came in with his crossed eyes and his lopsided grin and told her it was right nice, the barley, right nice. She gave him the soft heel of yesterday's loaf, because his teeth were bad and he would never say so.

Alek brayed at something one of the others said. She told him if he didn't lower his voice she would put him in charge of the mules. The table roared. Alek went red and laughed louder.

The room had a temperature that wasn't the fire. She had always felt it. When the men came in angry, it ran hot and close, like a kitchen in August. When they came in grieving, it ran cold and thin. She cooked for the temperature. More salt when it was cold. More fat when it was hot. She had never told anyone. It sounded mad.

This morning it was cold. It had been cold for a year.

Through the blurry window, she saw a girl go by in the gray light. Thin. Stringy hair. A shawl clutched at her throat. Nell, from the Staff and Flagon. Elspeth had seen her at the gallows the other day, standing beside the cart with that dead wizard on it. Nell had not looked away. Elspeth remembered that.

She set a bowl aside and covered it with a plate. She didn't know who for. Someone.

--

At midday, the pub went quiet.

It was not the miners. Most of them had gone back up the hill. It was everything else. The fire stopped crackling. The pots stopped bubbling. The ladle on its hook by the mantle swung a little, the way it did when someone brushed past it, and made no sound against the stone.

Elspeth stood in the middle of the floor with a stack of bowls in her arms.

The fire was still burning. She could see it. Every flame leaned the same way. Not up the flue. Sideways. Toward the window. Toward the mountain.

Toward the Pit.

She set the bowls down very carefully. She went to the window. The Blackvein peaks stood where they always stood. The sky was gray. Nothing moved. A crow sat on the smithy roof with its beak open and no sound coming out.

She put her hand on the glass. It was cold.

"Matthis," she said, and did not hear herself say it.

She stood there for what felt like a very long time. Then the crow cawed. The fire cracked. The pot boiled over and hissed on the coals, and she ran to it and swung the crane out and burned her wrist doing it, and swore one of Father's words, and the room was loud and ordinary again.

The flames stood straight up.

She held her wrist under cold water. She did not think about the window. She thought about supper. He would want supper. She thought about that very hard, all afternoon, the way you hold onto a rope.

--

He came at dusk.

She heard him stop outside. She heard something drop, soft, on the step. Then the latch.

He was black from his hair to his boots. Coal dust in his eyebrows, in the creases of his neck, in the corners of his mouth. He smelled like the inside of the mountain. And underneath that, for the first time in a year, he did not smell of death. He smelled of something else. Cold. Clean. Like snow before it falls.

She did not say anything. She pulled out the chair by the fire, not the bench in the corner, and pointed at it.

He sat.

She brought the stew. She brought the bowl she had covered at midday. It was still warm. She didn't think about that either.

He ate slowly this time. He held the spoon like it was heavy. When he reached for the bread she saw his hand. A splinter had gone in deep along the side of his finger, the skin swollen red around it.

"Give me that."

He held his hand out without arguing. That frightened her more than anything else that day. Matthis always argued.

She knelt in front of him and took his hand in both of hers. His fingers were cold. Not cold like a man who had been outside. Cold like a cellar. Like the inside of a stone. She held his hand between her palms and waited. She did not know what she was waiting for.

After a moment, his fingers warmed.

She took the needle from her apron pocket and worked the splinter loose. He did not flinch. When it came out she pressed the cloth to it and held it there.

"Did you find it?" she said. "What you were looking for?"

He looked at the fire for a long time.

"I don't know," he said. "Mine gas, maybe."

She nodded as though she believed him. She kept her hands where they were.

The fire crackled. The stew steamed. Somewhere outside, a dog barked, and stopped.

When he left, he forgot to leave coins. She was glad. She picked up his bowl and wiped the table where it had been.

The old oak shone under her apron like quartz.

She did not notice. She never did.
